Title: Nick and Me  
Credit: Written by  
Author: Author  
Source: Adapted from book  
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY  

NARRATOR  
Big brother was mine. Not that stupid book we had to read in junior high, but my real Big Brother.  

The camera pans around a modest fifth-floor apartment, cluttered with everyday items. A YOUNG MAN, late teens, sits at a small kitchen table, looking out the window.  

NARRATOR (CONT'D)  
Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.  

CUT TO:  

INT. GYM - DAY  

NARRATOR  
One time, at Moxie’s gym, a guy was doin’ preacher curls.  

NICK, a muscular young man with scars and messy reddish-brown hair, strides in. The gym-goer drops the barbell, intimidated.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Nick just nodded at him, then took over the place.  

NICK picks up a 50-pound bar, effortlessly curling it.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND APARTMENT - DAY  

NARRATOR  
We used to play kickball in the alley.  

YOUNG MAN and NICK play with a group of KIDS, laughing.  

NARRATOR (CONT'D)  
One time, little Mark Conner kicked a ball right through old Mrs. Engel’s kitchen.  

MARK kicks the ball, and it shatters a window. OLD MRS. ENGEL storms out, furious.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Nick stepped right up to her and said he was the one who did it.  

NICK stands tall, facing MRS. ENGEL, unflinching.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LATER  

NARRATOR  
Nick is a real-life Viking.  

YOUNG MAN looks at a family photo on the wall, showing their ancestors.  

NARRATOR (CONT'D)  
People respect Nick and our family, even though dad took off.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. DOWNTOWN MILWAUKEE - DAY  

NARRATOR  
Nick always goes in first to check out the bars.  

NICK walks confidently into a bar, and patrons clear out of his way.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He’s a crazy man.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

NARRATOR  
The other day he comes over to check on us.  

NICK stands in the doorway, concerned.  

NICK  
What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day?  

YOUNG MAN shrugs, looking worried.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
I needed to take better care of her.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KITCHEN - DAY  

NARRATOR  
Ma worked for years as a janitor.  

MA, an older woman with tired eyes, cleans the kitchen.  

NARRATOR (CONT'D)  
She doesn’t get much money every month.  

YOUNG MAN sits at the table, looking at bills.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. STREET - DAY  

NARRATOR  
I can’t afford to go back to school.  

YOUNG MAN rides his bike, deep in thought.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Nick says, “You don’t need a degree to get a good job.”  

CUT TO:  

INT. PIZZA PLACE - DAY  

NARRATOR  
This one day, I met a girl.  

YOUNG MAN walks into the pizza place and bumps into SIONNA, a pretty girl with short brown hair.  

YOUNG MAN  
Are you okay?  

SIONNA  
Yes.  

They share a moment, looking at each other.  

YOUNG MAN  
What’s your name?  

SIONNA  
Sionna.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

NARRATOR  
Ma was in a crazy mood.  

MA searches for her pain pills, mumbling.  

YOUNG MAN  
Ma, what’s the matter?  

MA looks confused, her eyes glassy.  

CUT TO:  

INT. BAR - NIGHT  

NARRATOR  
I went down to Joe’s to get a beer.  

YOUNG MAN sits at the bar, watching the game. NICK enters, angry.  

NICK  
What’s going on?  

YOUNG MAN  
Ma’s been confused.  

NICK  
Take her to the store or something!  

CUT TO:  

EXT. LIBRARY - NIGHT  

NARRATOR  
I walked to the library.  

YOUNG MAN enters, looking around in awe at the books.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
I started looking at all the books.  

He picks up "Wuthering Heights."  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

NARRATOR  
I started reading that book.  

YOUNG MAN sits on the couch, engrossed in the novel.  

NARRATOR (CONT'D)  
It reminded me of Sionna.  

CUT TO:  

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY  

NARRATOR  
We decided to meet downtown.  

SIONNA sits at a table, waiting. YOUNG MAN walks in, nervous.  

YOUNG MAN  
Hi, Sionna.  

SIONNA  
Hi!  

They smile at each other, a connection forming.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
She was so smart; even though smart isn’t the right word for it.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. PARK - DAY  

NARRATOR  
Sionna and I continued to meet regularly.  

They walk together, laughing and talking.  

NARRATOR (CONT'D)  
I started to really like reading.  

YOUNG MAN reads a book, intrigued.  

CUT TO:  

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY  

NARRATOR  
I found myself trying yoga.  

YOUNG MAN lies on a mat, struggling to keep up.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
I felt completely out of breath.  

SIONNA smiles at him encouragingly.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

NARRATOR  
Nick is pissed off.  

NICK paces, angry.  

NICK  
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment!  

YOUNG MAN  
I was with someone!  

NICK storms out, slamming the door.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
What a mess this was.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

NARRATOR  
One time, back when Nick was in high school, he got so mad at a kid named Steve.  

YOUNG MAN sits on the couch, recalling the memory.  

NARRATOR (CONT'D)  
Steve said something mean to Nick’s girlfriend, and she came to him crying.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. STEVE'S HOUSE - NIGHT  

NICK, furious, stands outside Steve's house, a can of lighter fluid in hand.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Nick went over to that kid’s house in the middle of the night.  

NICK sprays lighter fluid around the front yard, his face twisted in anger.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
He set that crap on fire!  

The flames ignite, illuminating NICK's face as he watches, satisfied.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

YOUNG MAN shakes his head, conflicted.  

NARRATOR  
I couldn’t believe it. He got away with it too.  

CUT TO:  

INT. LIBRARY - DAY  

YOUNG MAN browses the shelves, lost in thought.  

NARRATOR  
I was trying to figure out how to make all the people in my life fit together.  

He picks up a book on yoga, then glances at a book on Buddhism.  

NARRATOR (CONT'D)  
Ma, Nick, Sionna; they are all so different.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

YOUNG MAN sits at the kitchen table, talking on the phone.  

YOUNG MAN  
I wish you would come over to my place, meet my Ma.  

SIONNA (V.O.)  
Are you sure? It’s kind of a big deal to meet your mother.  

YOUNG MAN  
She probably won’t even remember meeting you.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

YOUNG MAN hangs up the phone, a smile on his face.  

NARRATOR  
I felt like I was growing up.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

YOUNG MAN sits with MA, who is watching a game show.  

YOUNG MAN  
How’s it goin’ Ma?  

MA  
Oh, I don’t know. It just feels like my heart is going to jump out of my chest!  

YOUNG MAN's expression shifts to concern.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
I didn’t like the sound of that.  

CUT TO:  

INT. DOCTOR'S OFFICE - DAY  

YOUNG MAN sits in a chair, anxious.  

NARRATOR  
I called Dr. Carson.  

DR. CARSON enters, serious.  

DR. CARSON  
Your mother’s blood pressure is dangerously high.  

YOUNG MAN  
What’s the matter with it?  

DR. CARSON  
I’m afraid of the risk of a stroke.  

CUT TO:  

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY  

MA lies in a hospital bed, looking frail.  

NARRATOR  
They only kept Ma in the hospital for three days.  

SIONNA enters, brightening the room.  

NARRATOR (CONT'D)  
Sionna met me at the hospital one day for lunch.  

They walk outside, the sun shining.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Her eyes sparkled like diamonds.  

CUT TO:  

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT  

YOUNG MAN falls asleep in a chair, dreaming.  

NARRATOR  
In the dream, I was a soldier.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. BATTLEFIELD - NIGHT  

Chaos surrounds YOUNG MAN as he stands over a fallen soldier.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
I felt exhausted and sad.  

NICK appears, running towards him.  

NICK  
Thank God you’re here!  

Suddenly, NICK stabs YOUNG MAN in the chest.  

NARRATOR (V.O.)  
Nick had killed me.  

CUT TO:  

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT  

YOUNG MAN wakes up, gasping for breath.  

NARRATOR  
I felt betrayed and confused.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

YOUNG MAN prepares to take MA home.  

NARRATOR  
I was going to be responsible for keeping Ma alive.  

SIONNA helps him organize MA's medications.  

NARRATOR (CONT'D)  
For the first time, things were starting to go right.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

NICK storms in, face red with rage.  

NICK  
What’s up little Bro?  

YOUNG MAN  
I got Ma all tucked in.  

NICK laughs mockingly.  

NARRATOR  
His smirk was starting to really piss me off.  

YOUNG MAN  
Sionna helped me figure it out.  

NICK  
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.  

YOUNG MAN  
Stop it, Nick!  

NICK lunges forward, anger boiling over.  

NICK  
Don’t you EVER talk that way about MY mother!  

YOUNG MAN  
You’re so Goddamn hateful!  

NICK slaps YOUNG MAN across the head and storms out.  

NARRATOR  
I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

YOUNG MAN sits at the kitchen table, staring blankly at a pile of bills.  

NARRATOR  
The weight of responsibility was heavy.  

MA enters, looking frail but determined.  

MA  
What’s wrong, dear?  

YOUNG MAN  
Just... trying to figure things out.  

MA  
You’re doing your best.  

YOUNG MAN forces a smile.  

YOUNG MAN  
Yeah, I know.  

NARRATOR  
But it felt like I was drowning.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. CITY STREET - DAY  

YOUNG MAN walks with SIONNA, who holds his hand tightly.  

NARRATOR  
Sionna was my anchor.  

SIONNA  
You need to talk to Nick.  

YOUNG MAN  
I don’t want to.  

SIONNA  
He’s still your brother.  

YOUNG MAN stops, looking conflicted.  

YOUNG MAN  
What if he just makes things worse?  

SIONNA  
You won’t know until you try.  

NARRATOR  
Her words lingered in the air.  

CUT TO:  

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT  

YOUNG MAN paces the living room, phone in hand.  

NARRATOR  
I had to make the call.  

He dials NICK's number, anxiety building.  

YOUNG MAN  
(voice trembling)  
Nick... we need to talk.  

NICK (V.O.)  
What do you want?  

YOUNG MAN  
I want to fix things between us.  

NICK (V.O.)  
You think I care?  

YOUNG MAN  
Please, just listen.  

NARRATOR  
But I knew he wouldn’t.  

CUT TO:  

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT  

YOUNG MAN sits beside MA, who is asleep.  

NARRATOR  
I had to be strong for her.  

He looks at her peaceful face, determination in his eyes.  

YOUNG MAN  
I won’t let you down, Ma.  

NARRATOR  
I would fight for her, no matter what.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. BATTLEFIELD - NIGHT  

YOUNG MAN stands alone, haunted by memories.  

NARRATOR  
The past was a ghost that wouldn’t let go.  

He clenches his fists, ready to confront his demons.  

NARRATOR (CONT'D)  
I had to face Nick, for Ma’s sake.  

FADE OUT.  

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - NIGHT  

NICK sits on the couch, staring blankly at the wall. The room is cluttered with empty bottles and takeout containers.  

NARRATOR  
Nick had been distant lately, lost in his own world.  

The door creaks open, and YOUNG MAN enters cautiously.  

YOUNG MAN  
Hey, Nick.  

NICK  
(turning slowly)  
What do you want?  

YOUNG MAN  
I just wanted to check on you.  

NICK  
(scoffing)  
Check on me? You think I need checking on?  

YOUNG MAN  
I’m just worried, man.  

NICK  
(silence)  
Worried about what?  

YOUNG MAN  
About you. About Ma.  

NICK  
(sighs)  
I’m fine.  

YOUNG MAN  
You don’t look fine.  

NICK  
What do you want me to say? That I’m a mess?  

YOUNG MAN  
No, I just want you to talk to me.  

NICK  
Why? So you can judge me?  

YOUNG MAN  
No! I just want to help.  

NICK  
Help? You think you can help me?  

YOUNG MAN  
I’m trying, Nick.  

NICK  
(standing up)  
You don’t know anything about me!  

YOUNG MAN  
I know you’re my brother.  

NICK  
(softening)  
Yeah, well…  

YOUNG MAN  
Let’s just talk, okay?  

NICK  
Fine.  

CUT TO:  

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - NIGHT  

SIONNA and YOUNG MAN sit on the couch, the atmosphere lighter.  

NARRATOR  
After the confrontation, I needed solace.  

SIONNA  
How did it go?  

YOUNG MAN  
(sighs)  
Not great.  

SIONNA  
You did the right thing.  

YOUNG MAN  
I hope so.  

NARRATOR  
I wanted to believe that.  

They share a quiet moment, leaning into each other.  

NARRATOR (CONT'D)  
But the weight of my choices lingered.  

FADE OUT.  

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY  

YOUNG MAN stands at the front desk, anxiety etched on his face. OFFICER DUPUIS approaches him.  

OFFICER DUPUIS  
Mr. Olson, thank you for coming in.  

YOUNG MAN  
What’s going on with Nick?  

OFFICER DUPUIS  
We need to discuss the accident.  

YOUNG MAN  
Is he going to be okay?  

OFFICER DUPUIS  
He’s stable, but there are serious charges pending.  

YOUNG MAN  
Charges?  

OFFICER DUPUIS  
Vehicular manslaughter.  

YOUNG MAN  
He didn’t mean to...  

OFFICER DUPUIS  
I understand, but the law is the law.  

YOUNG MAN  
What can I do?  

OFFICER DUPUIS  
You can support him. That’s all he needs right now.  

YOUNG MAN nods, determination rising within him.  

CUT TO:  

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY  

YOUNG MAN enters, the place dim and disheveled. He looks around, memories flooding back.  

NARRATOR  
This was once a home filled with laughter, now it felt like a tomb.  

He picks up a framed photo of NICK and their mother, a wave of grief washing over him.  

YOUNG MAN  
(whispering)  
What have you done, Nick?  

He sets the photo down, a mix of anger and sorrow in his eyes.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. CEMETERY - DAY  

YOUNG MAN stands at OLIVIA'S grave, the autumn leaves swirling around him. He kneels, placing a few flowers on the grave.  

YOUNG MAN  
I’m sorry, Ma.  

NARRATOR  
Grief is a strange companion, always lurking just beneath the surface.  

He wipes away a tear, looking up at the sky.  

YOUNG MAN  
I’ll take care of him, I promise.  

He stands, a newfound resolve in his posture.  

CUT TO:  

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY  

NICK lies in bed, bruised and battered. YOUNG MAN enters, the tension thick in the air.  

NICK  
Hey, bro.  

YOUNG MAN  
Hey.  

NICK looks away, guilt written all over his face.  

NICK  
I didn’t mean for any of this to happen.  

YOUNG MAN  
I know.  

NICK  
I just wanted to take her to church.  

YOUNG MAN  
You need to face the consequences, Nick.  

NICK  
I can’t. I can’t deal with this.  

YOUNG MAN  
You have to.  

NICK looks up, desperation in his eyes.  

NICK  
What if I go to jail?  

YOUNG MAN  
Then we’ll figure it out together.  

NICK nods, tears welling up.  

NICK  
I’m so sorry.  

YOUNG MAN  
I know. Just... be strong.  

They share a moment of understanding, the bond of brotherhood still intact despite the pain.  

CUT TO:  

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - NIGHT  

SIONNA and YOUNG MAN sit together, the weight of the world on their shoulders.  

SIONNA  
You’re doing the right thing.  

YOUNG MAN  
I hope so.  

SIONNA  
You’re not alone in this.  

YOUNG MAN  
I know.  

NARRATOR  
In the darkest moments, love can be the light that guides us through.  

They hold hands, a silent promise of support and resilience.  

CUT TO:  

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - NIGHT  

NICK paces the small, cluttered living room, his face twisted in anger and frustration. He glances at the clock, tapping his foot impatiently.  

NICK  
(voice raised)  
Where the hell is Bob?  

He picks up a half-empty bottle of whiskey from the coffee table and takes a swig, grimacing at the burn.  

NARRATOR  
In moments of despair, the mind can spiral into darkness, seeking solace in the very vices that lead to ruin.  

NICK throws the bottle against the wall, shattering it. He runs a hand through his hair, breathing heavily.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. BUS STOP - NIGHT  

NICK stands alone at the bus stop, the cold wind biting at his skin. He watches as the bus approaches, headlights cutting through the darkness.  

NARRATOR  
The night was heavy with regret, each breath a reminder of the choices that led him here.  

NICK boards the bus, his expression hardening as he takes a seat in the back, staring out the window.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - NIGHT  

NICK steps off the bus and approaches SIONNA's house, his fists clenched. He hesitates at the door, taking a deep breath before knocking forcefully.  

SIONNA  
(voice from inside)  
Who is it?  

NICK  
It's me. Open up.  

The door creaks open, revealing SIONNA, her expression a mix of concern and fear.  

SIONNA  
Nick, what’s wrong?  

NICK pushes past her, entering the living room.  

NICK  
I need to talk to you.  

SIONNA  
(uneasy)  
Okay... about what?  

NICK  
(voice low)  
About your boyfriend.  

SIONNA  
What about him?  

NICK  
He’s the reason my life is falling apart.  

SIONNA  
(defensive)  
That’s not fair, Nick.  

NICK  
(angry)  
Not fair? You don’t know what I’ve been through!  

SIONNA  
I know you’re hurting, but—  

NICK  
(interrupting)  
But nothing! He needs to stay away from my brother.  

SIONNA  
(pleading)  
You can’t just come in here and—  

NICK  
(voice rising)  
Watch me!  

NARRATOR  
In his rage, he saw only the enemy, blind to the love that surrounded him.  

CUT TO:  

INT. COURTROOM - DAY  

NICK sits at a table, his lawyer, DANIEL DAWSON, beside him. The atmosphere is tense, filled with murmurs from the gallery.  

NARRATOR  
The weight of judgment hung in the air, each tick of the clock a reminder of the impending verdict.  

The JUDGE, a stern woman, enters, and the room falls silent.  

JUDGE  
Mr. Olson, you stand accused of involuntary manslaughter. How do you plead?  

NICK  
(voice trembling)  
Not guilty.  

NARRATOR  
In that moment, he felt the eyes of the world upon him, a spotlight on his darkest hour.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. COURTHOUSE - DAY  

NICK exits the courthouse, flanked by his lawyer. Reporters shout questions, cameras flashing.  

REPORTER  
Nick! How do you feel about the charges?  

NICK  
(voice strained)  
I just want to go home.  

NARRATOR  
But home felt like a distant memory, a place lost to the shadows of his choices.  

NICK pushes through the crowd, determination etched on his face.  

CUT TO:  

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - NIGHT  

NICK sits on the floor, surrounded by empty bottles and crumpled papers. He stares blankly at the wall, lost in thought.  

NARRATOR  
In the depths of despair, he realized that the only way out was through the pain he had long avoided.  

He picks up a pen and begins to write, the words flowing as he confronts his demons.  

NICK  
(voiceover)  
I can’t keep running. I have to face this.  

FADE OUT.  

INT. FERRY - DAY  

The ferry glides across the gray water. NARRATOR stands on the second floor, coffee and burrito in hand, observing the bustling crowd.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
The ferry is a familiar place, a bridge between my past and present.  

He watches as PEOPLE line up for coffee, the warmth of the burrito radiating in his palm.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
I feel the heat of the burrito, a small comfort in a world that often feels cold.  

Suddenly, a SMALL BOY slips over the banister. NARRATOR lunges forward, grabbing the boy's ankle just in time.  

NARRATOR  
(panicked)  
Hold on!  

The boy hangs limply, and time slows. NARRATOR struggles to pull him up, fear gripping him.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
In that moment, I realized I was both the savior and the one in peril.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. SEATTLE STREETS - DAY  

NARRATOR walks through the slick sidewalks of Seattle, the mist hanging in the air. He observes the PEOPLE around him, each wrapped in their own world.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
The Seattle freeze. A city of barriers, where emotions are kept at bay.  

He passes a STREET MUSICIAN playing an electric keyboard, the sound echoing through the damp air.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
Music, a fleeting connection in a city that often feels disconnected.  

CUT TO:  

INT. LOWELL'S RESTAURANT - DAY  

NARRATOR sits at a table, gazing out at the Puget Sound. He orders blueberry French toast, lost in thought.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
I miss her. Siona. The warmth of her smile, the comfort of her presence.  

He watches the TOURISTS outside, their laughter a stark contrast to his somber mood.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
Seven years since my life changed forever. Seven years of loneliness.  

CUT TO:  

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - NIGHT  

NARRATOR sits on the floor, surrounded by memories. He picks up a photo of Siona, tears welling in his eyes.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
I thought I could escape the past, but it clings to me like a shadow.  

He puts the photo down, determination replacing despair.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
But I must face it. I must confront my demons.  

CUT TO:  

INT. PRISON VISITING ROOM - DAY  

NARRATOR sits across from NICK, the tension palpable. NICK looks older, worn down by his choices.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
This is it. The moment I've waited for.  

NICK's eyes are filled with regret as he speaks.  

NICK  
I’m sorry for everything.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
Forgiveness. A gift I never thought I could give.  

NARRATOR leans forward, sincerity in his voice.  

NARRATOR  
I forgive you, Nick.  

NICK's tears flow freely, a release of years of pain.  

NICK  
I didn’t expect this.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
In that moment, I felt the weight of thirty years lift from my shoulders.  

CUT TO:  

EXT. AIRPORT - DAY  

NARRATOR walks through the bustling airport, a sense of freedom washing over him. He smiles, feeling lighter.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
Time moves forward, and so must I.  

He boards the plane, the attendant scanning his boarding pass.  

NARRATOR  
(voiceover)  
I am Peter Olson. I am free.  

FADE OUT.  

THE END.
